== 1. Identity
<identity>
=== 1.1 Substance
<substance>
Tin is the chemical element with symbol Sn (from Latin #emph[stannum])
and atomic number 50. Among the dhatus, tin has distinctive
practical-chemical properties:

- #strong[Low melting point]: 231.93°C — lowest of any classical dhatu;
  easily worked
- #strong[High boiling point]: 2602°C — does not significantly
  volatilize during typical processing
- #strong[Reactivity]: relatively unreactive to most acids; reacts
  slowly with oxygen
- #strong[Allotropic transition]: gray (alpha) tin → white (beta) tin at
  13.2°C — relevant in cold climates ("tin pest")
- #strong[Forms intermetallics]: with copper (bronze series), with lead
  (solder), with antimony (Britannia metal); the Sn-Cu intermetallics
  are central to Indian bronze tradition
- #strong[Coatings]: tin plates iron to make tinplate; protects against
  corrosion

Physical properties: - Density: 7.31 g/cm³ at 20°C (β-tin) — nearly
identical to iron’s 7.87 - Melting point: 231.93°C (much lower than
other dhatus) - Color: silvery-white metallic - Hardness: 1.5 Mohs (very
soft, even softer than gold) - BCT (body-centered tetragonal) crystal
structure for β-tin; cubic for α-tin

Tin’s softness and low melting point make it the easiest of the
classical dhatus to work with mechanically — a property classical
metallurgists noted and exploited.

=== 1.2 Nomenclature
<nomenclature>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] and the #emph[Charaka Samhita], tin’s
Sanskrit names include:

- #strong[Vanga] (वङ्ग) — most common technical term; derives from
  regional name (Bengal/eastern India source)
- #strong[Trapu] (त्रपु) — older term for tin; cognate with related
  Indo-European words
- #strong[Ranga] (रङ्ग) — "color/dye" reference; tin was used
  historically in dye preparation
- #strong[Picudara] (पिचुदार) — literal meaning unclear, possibly
  referring to tin’s softness
- #strong[Misra-loha] (मिश्र-लोह) — "mixed metal" reference, possibly to
  tin’s role as alloy component
- #strong[Devadhatu] (देवधातु) — "deity-metal," indicating ritual/sacred
  status
- #strong[Sweta-loha] (श्वेत-लोह) — "white metal"
- #strong[Sukshma-loha] (सूक्ष्म-लोह) — "subtle/fine metal," referring to
  tin’s malleability

=== 1.3 The two classical types of tin
<the-two-classical-types-of-tin>
Classical texts distinguish two primary types of tin based on physical
characteristics and utility:

#strong[1. Khuraka Vanga] (खुरक वङ्ग) — "hoof tin"; harder, suitable for
medical use after processing #strong[2. Mishraka Vanga] (मिश्रक वङ्ग) —
"mixed tin"; softer, often containing lead or antimony as impurity; less
suitable for medical use without thorough purification

This distinction reflects practical knowledge: tin from different
sources varies significantly in purity, with lead and antimony being
common natural contaminants. Properly identified Khuraka Vanga
represents the higher-quality starting material for medicinal
preparations.

=== 1.4 Graha correspondence
<graha-correspondence>
Tin corresponds to #strong[Guru] (Jupiter) in the classical Indian
astrological framework. This correspondence is unanimous across Indian
primary sources: - #emph[Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra] (BPHS) Chapter 3
- #emph[Brihat Samhita] (Varahamihira) on planetary metallurgy -
#emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] graha-rasa identifications - #emph[Charaka
Samhita] on therapeutic correspondences

#strong[The cross-tradition note]:

Western Hermetic alchemy: - Tin → Jupiter

Classical Indian jyotisha: - Tin → Guru (Jupiter)

#strong[For tin specifically, the Western and Indian conventions agree.]
This is one of the dhatu correspondences where Indian and Western
alchemical traditions converge — likely because both traditions
identified tin’s softness, malleability, beneficial alloying properties,
and association with abundance with Jupiter-quality.

This is in contrast to iron (Volume IV) and copper (Volume V) where the
conventions diverge significantly. The agreement on tin (and to a lesser
extent gold, where both correspond to the Sun) suggests that some
metal-graha correspondences may be more "objectively" observable than
others — color and chemical reactivity may be more universally
recognizable than war-quality vs structural-quality.

Within the dhatu/jeeva/moola classification: - #strong[Guru is
jeeva-graha] — the "living/cognitive" class (same as Surya-gold and
Budha-mercury)

Friendship pattern of Guru in BPHS naisargika: - Guru is friend of:
Surya (Sun), Chandra (Moon), Mangala (Mars) - Guru is enemy of: Budha
(Mercury), Shukra (Venus) - Guru is neutral to: Shani (Saturn), Rahu,
Ketu

The #emph[enemy of Budha and Shukra] status is structurally significant.
Tin operations involving mercury (Budha) face graha-tension; tin
operations involving silver (Shukra in some traditions) face similar
tension. Tin operations involving copper (Mangala) are well-supported by
friendship — #emph[which is why bronze tradition flourished
structurally].

=== 1.5 Origins and Sources
<origins-and-sources>
Classical Indian tin sources: - #strong[Bengal region] — the
etymological source of "Vanga"; tin was historically traded through
Bengal ports - #strong[South-East Asia trade routes] — Burma, Thailand,
Malaysia provided imported tin - #strong[Cornwall (England)] —
eventually a trade source via Roman-period exchange networks -
#strong[Local minor deposits] — Bihar, Andhra Pradesh have small
cassiterite deposits

India was historically a tin importer rather than primary producer. The
trade routes for tin (alongside spices and silk) were significant
economic arteries connecting India with Southeast Asia and beyond.

