== 2. The Shodhana–Marana Sequence
<the-shodhanamarana-sequence>
=== 2.1 Shodhana (Purification)
<shodhana-purification>
Tin’s purification follows a modified version of the standard sequence,
reflecting tin’s lower melting point and different reactivity:

#strong[Procedure:] 1. #strong[Taila (sesame oil)] — tin foil heated to
~400-500°C (well below its 232°C melting point at room pressure but
raised by heat in oxidizing atmosphere) and quenched in oil 7 times 2.
#strong[Takra (buttermilk)] — quenched 7 times 3. #strong[Go-mutra
(cow’s urine)] — quenched 7 times 4. #strong[Aranala (fermented kanji)]
— quenched 7 times 5. #strong[Kulattha-kvatha (horse-gram decoction)] —
quenched 7 times

#strong[Tin-specific consideration]: tin’s low melting point (232°C)
means heating must be carefully controlled during shodhana. Too-high
temperatures cause melting and loss of mass; too-low temperatures fail
to drive surface oxidation. The classical heating regime appears to
balance these factors using empirical knowledge transmitted across
generations.

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

Each of the five quenching media interacts with tin’s surface
differently: - #strong[Sesame oil]: organic medium provides reducing
atmosphere during cooling; prevents excessive surface oxidation -
#strong[Buttermilk]: lactic acid dissolves any tin hydroxide that forms
(tin reacts slowly with water) - #strong[Cow urine]: alkaline NH₃
environment; tin forms soluble stannate complexes in alkaline media
(this can be problematic if too aggressive) - #strong[Kanji]: acidic,
removes residual surface contamination - #strong[Kulattha]: amino-acid
mediated surface conditioning

The cycling helps remove lead and antimony contaminants (the main
concerns for #emph[Mishraka Vanga]). Lead reacts with the various media
in different ways than tin, allowing differential removal.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Standard procedure across licensed
Ayurvedic pharmacies.

=== 2.2 Jarana (Heating and Stirring) — tin-specific
<jarana-heating-and-stirring-tin-specific>
#strong[This step is tin-specific] and not present in most other dhatu
protocols. The classical procedure includes: - Purified tin is melted
with extensive stirring in an open vessel - Various herbal additions are
made (typically including #emph[aswagandha], #emph[triphala], sometimes
#emph[haridra]) - The molten tin is held at temperature for extended
periods (multiple hours) - Visible foam and "scoria" floating on the
surface is repeatedly skimmed off

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

Jarana is #emph[zone refining] of tin. The molten metal at moderate
temperature has different solubility for different impurities; vigorous
stirring brings impurities to the surface where they are removed as
scoria (oxide + slag). The herbal additions provide: - Reducing agents
(organic compounds prevent excessive tin oxidation) - Surface-active
compounds (helping bring impurities to the surface) - Possibly chelating
agents (binding lead and other heavy metals)

Modern characterization confirms that jarana significantly reduces lead,
copper, and iron impurities in the tin — typically to \<1% from initial
levels of 5-10%.

=== 2.3 Marana (Incineration)
<marana-incineration>
The transformation from purified tin to #emph[vanga-bhasma] requires
multiple putas and produces SnO₂ (tin(IV) oxide, cassiterite phase).

#strong[Procedure (per multiple modern characterizations):] 1.
#emph[Shuddha Vanga] (purified, jarana-processed tin) is rolled/cut into
thin pieces 2. Triturated with mercury and sulfur (kajjali-mediated
route) OR with pure herbal extracts (mercury-free route) 3. Cakes formed
and dried 4. Sealed in #emph[sharava-samputa] 5. Subjected to
#emph[puta] heating cycles 6. Modern preparations may use electric
muffle furnace at 600°C for 3 hours per cycle

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

Tin’s oxidation under puta conditions:

#strong[Stage 1]: Sn (metallic, +0) + ½O₂ → SnO (stannous oxide, +2) -
SnO is metastable; black colored - Forms initially during
low-temperature oxidation

#strong[Stage 2]: 2SnO + O₂ → 2SnO₂ (stannic oxide / cassiterite, +4) -
SnO₂ is thermodynamically stable; white-grey colored - Forms
preferentially at puta temperatures (\>500°C) with extended heating

The classical procedure achieves complete conversion to SnO₂ through
repeated heating cycles. Each puta: - Heating drives further oxidation
(Sn⁰ → Sn²⁺ → Sn⁴⁺) - Cooling allows phase stabilization - Trituration
breaks up agglomerates and exposes fresh surface - Repeated cycles
produce nano-scale particles

#strong[The traditional vs. modern heating method comparison] (from
Patil et al.):

