#strong[Convergent finding]: traditional vanga-bhasma is #strong[SnO₂
(cassiterite, tetragonal phase) at the nanoscale], with particle-size
distribution dependent on processing method. The chemistry is consistent
with classical specifications; the particle-size variation between
methods is a refinement detail not articulated in classical texts.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Multiple modern studies confirm
consistent SnO₂ phase identity across batches and methods.

=== 2.4 The traditional confirmation tests
<the-traditional-confirmation-tests>
The standard tests apply: #emph[rekhapurnatva], #emph[varitaratva],
#emph[apunarbhava], #emph[niruttha], #emph[nirdhuma], #emph[niswadu],
#emph[nischandratva]. For tin specifically: - #emph[Rekhapurnatva]
particularly important — confirms particle size \<10 μm -
#emph[Varitaratva] — confirms ultrafine, hydrophobic surface -
#emph[Niruttha] — confirms thorough oxidation to SnO₂ (no residual Sn⁰
that would interact with mercury)

Modern XRD validation confirms what these tests measure indirectly. The
classical QC system was empirically calibrated to detect proper
conversion through observable proxies that correlate with modern
instrumental analysis.

