== 3. Major Tin Preparations
<major-tin-preparations>
=== 3.1 Vanga-Bhasma (Tin Calx / Tin Oxide Nanoparticles)
<vanga-bhasma-tin-calx-tin-oxide-nanoparticles>
#strong[Composition:] SnO₂ (cassiterite phase, tetragonal); some SnO may
persist in incompletely processed batches \
#strong[Color:] White-grey to pale grey \
#strong[Particle size:] 50-300 nm depending on processing method \
#strong[Source text:] Rasaratna Samuccaya, Bhaishajya Ratnavali, Rasa
Tarangini, Bhavaprakasha

#strong[Therapeutic indications (classical):] - #emph[Prameha] (diabetes
\/ urinary disorders) — primary indication - #emph[Mutra-roga] (urinary
tract conditions) - #emph[Shukrameha] (spermatorrhea) - #emph[Klaibya]
(impotence) - #emph[Vajikarana] — vitalization, especially for male
reproductive support - #emph[Kasa] (cough), #emph[Shvasa] (asthma) —
respiratory conditions - #emph[Krimi] (parasitic infections) -
#emph[Pandu] (anemia, in combinations)

#strong[Modern pharmacology research:]

Vanga-bhasma is moderately well-researched compared to other dhatus: -
#strong[Anti-diabetic activity]: Multiple animal studies show blood
glucose reduction - #strong[Anti-microbial]: documented against gut
pathogens - #strong[Anti-fungal]: some activity against Candida species
- #strong[Anti-cancer]: preliminary studies show activity against
various cell lines - #strong[Bioaccessibility]: Patil et al. 2018
specifically measured gastrointestinal vs gastric digestion
bioaccessibility, finding higher bioaccessibility in intestinal
conditions — useful for predicting absorption

#strong[Dosage:] 30-125 mg twice daily classical; modern licensed
Ayurvedic practice typically 60-125 mg daily under physician supervision

#strong[Toxicity:] Tin in elemental and SnO₂ forms has very low toxicity
(tin metal is used in food cans without significant migration concerns).
SnO₂ nanoparticles have been studied for safety: - Generally low
toxicity in animal models - Some concern about long-term accumulation in
chronic high-dose use - No specific organ toxicity established at
recommended doses - Better safety profile than mercury or
lead-containing preparations

The toxicity profile is favorable — comparable to iron and superior to
mercury or copper preparations.

=== 3.2 Vanga-Sindhura (Tin Vermillion)
<vanga-sindhura-tin-vermillion>
#strong[Composition:] Mercury-mediated tin preparation containing HgS,
SnS₂, and tin-mercury-sulfur intermediate phases \
#strong[Use:] Specific therapeutic indications, especially for chronic
respiratory conditions

#strong[Classical preparation]: Tin is processed with mercury and sulfur
in kupipakwa apparatus, similar to makaradhwaja (Volume I §4.4) but with
tin replacing the gold component. The product retains mercury rather
than driving it off completely.

This preparation is graha-complex: tin (Guru) + mercury (Budha) + sulfur
(Mangala). Guru-Budha is #emph[enemy]; Guru-Mangala is #emph[friend];
Budha-Mangala is #emph[enemy]. Multiple tensions create complex timing
requirements (see §5).

=== 3.3 Vanga in Sacred Alloys
<vanga-in-sacred-alloys>
Tin appears as a primary component in #strong[panchaloha] (typically
4-5% by mass) and #strong[ashtadhatu] (12.5% by mass, equal-weighted).
Tin’s contribution: - Adds Guru-graha to the multi-metal cosmological
integration - Guru’s friendship with Surya, Chandra, Mangala provides
supportive integration with gold, silver, copper - Guru’s enmity with
Budha (mercury) and Shukra creates specific avoidance constraints in
multi-metal compositions

Cross-reference Volume V §3.3 for tin’s role in copper-tin alloys
(bronze series, Aranmula delta bronze, bell-bronze beta phase).

=== 3.4 Specialized Bronze Preparations
<specialized-bronze-preparations>
Tin’s most culturally significant role is as the alloy partner in
bronze. Volume V §4 details Aranmula kannadi (32.6% Sn, delta phase) and
bell-bronze (22-24% Sn, beta phase). These are tin-copper preparations
whose framework predictions are detailed in Volume V §5.5.

#strong[For tin’s perspective] (graha-Guru viewpoint): - Aranmula
kannadi: tin (Guru, 32.6% by mass) + copper (Mangala, 67.4%) —
Guru-Mangala friend; favorable framework predictions - Bell-bronze: tin
(Guru, 22-24% by mass) + copper (Mangala, 76-78%) — same Guru-Mangala
friendship; favorable framework predictions

These are among the most stable and successful classical metallurgical
traditions, partly because of the underlying graha-friendship between
component metals.

