== 5. Gap Analysis: Tradition vs Modern Interpretation
<gap-analysis-tradition-vs-modern-interpretation>
=== 5.1 Where the tradition is correct
<where-the-tradition-is-correct>
- #strong[Tin should be processed to oxide form for medicinal use] —
  accurately observed; SnO₂ is the classical end-state and modern
  characterization confirms this
- #strong[Tin requires jarana (zone refining) due to natural impurities]
  — accurately observed; lead and antimony contamination is common in
  raw tin
- #strong[Different tin sources have different qualities] — accurately
  observed; Khuraka vs Mishraka distinction matches modern understanding
  of tin ore variability
- #strong[Tin has anti-diabetic and respiratory therapeutic value] —
  supported by modern research
- #strong[Tin-Guru (Jupiter) correspondence] — converges with Western
  tradition; both classical traditions assigned tin to Jupiter
- #strong[The puta sequence achieves nano-particle SnO₂] — accurately
  observed
- #strong[Bronze tradition (tin-copper alloys) produces specific
  intermetallic phases] — fully validated; Volume V details bell-bronze
  and Aranmula

=== 5.2 Where the tradition’s claims need qualification
<where-the-traditions-claims-need-qualification>
- #strong[Specific clinical efficacy claims beyond diabetes and
  respiratory] — many secondary indications need rigorous study
- #strong["All vanga-bhasma is equivalent"] — particle-size variations
  between traditional and modern preparation methods are significant
- #strong[Some classical preparations using mercury-mediated route] —
  the mercury-mediated preparations (vanga-sindhura) are chemically
  distinct from pure SnO₂ vanga-bhasma; should not be conflated

=== 5.3 Where the framework can help
<where-the-framework-can-help>
- #strong[Tin-mercury-sulfur operations have most restrictive timing] in
  the dhatu compendium due to multiple graha-enmities; framework
  surfaces this clearly
- #strong[Tin-copper operations are favorable across many nakshatras] —
  supports the success of bronze traditions
