== 9. Closing Notes
<closing-notes>
Tin is the dhatu where Indian and Western alchemical traditions agree on
graha correspondence (both assign Jupiter). This convergence may reflect
that tin’s properties — softness, malleability, beneficial alloying
contribution, low melting point, association with abundance — are
universally observable rather than culture-specific.

Vanga-bhasma is methodologically well-studied because direct
experimental comparison exists between traditional gajaputa heating and
electric muffle furnace methods. This makes vanga-bhasma a useful model
system for any framework-validation studies on processing-method
variation effects.

The framework predictions for tin-mercury-sulfur operations are the most
restrictive in the dhatu compendium due to multiple graha-enmities
(Guru-Budha, Budha-Mangala simultaneously in the system). The
Pushya-as-stable-anchor pattern continues to emerge as the optimal
timing window across multi-graha mercurial operations.

Tin’s role as the alloy partner in bronze (Volume V §4) provides the
most empirically accessible validation pathway in the entire compendium:
bell-bronze acoustic analysis can be performed with minimal equipment
cost and tests the Guru-Mangala friendship prediction directly.

